;; TOOL-RFC-001, the verified core of the version gate.
;;
;; The RFC is docs/design/tool-rfc-001-version-gate.md. This module exists for
;; TOOL-ORACLE-1: this port passed `--strict-verified-core` VACUOUSLY, with zero
;; body-faithful functions, so that flag decided nothing about it. A gate whose
;; only instrument is "it agrees with itself" is a green light and not a proof.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT PART OF THE PORT. A `def` cannot call a
;; sibling `def` in the same module: the failure is at check time, before
;; verification, and no ordering escapes it. Cross-module calls to an
;; already-verified callee do work, leaf-first. So the module split is forced by
;; the language rather than chosen for tidiness, and the port imports this. Same
;; reason as tools/doc-archive/adjudicate.llmll and tools/doc-path-lint/.
;;
;; WHAT IS PROVABLE HERE AND WHAT IS NOT, and the boundary is not where a reader
;; would first guess. The gate's ORDER is what TOOL-RFC-001 pins: fourteen
;; conditions in the shell reference's sequence, "same order, same messages,
;; same exit codes". That order is NOT proved here, and it is not a criterion
;; ordering: measured on this file, the criterion labels interleave, with C2 at
;; positions 3 and 7 among C1s. Proving it would need a fourteen-boolean
;; function over a fifteen-constructor enum, which is a rewrite of a live gate
;; whose messages are compared byte for byte. That is out of scope here and is
;; recorded so the next reader does not mistake this file for it.
;;
;; Every recognizer is also out. `semver?`, `version-in` and `heading-version`
;; are bool- or string-valued bodies over string comparison, which fall to
;; erBodyFallback (STRLIT-BODY-1) and carry no proof.
;;
;; WHAT IS PROVED IS THE EXIT STATUS, which is the whole of what this gate hands
;; to CI. The range refinement is what stops a silent success: POSIX truncates to
;; the low 8 bits, so an unclamped 256 would exit 0 and report a PASS over a
;; failing tree. The three implications pin the policy and not merely the range,
;; because a body that clamped in the wrong direction would satisfy the range
;; and still fail open.
;;
;; MEASURED at v0.16.2: this module verifies SAFE and reports
;; `body-faithful: status-of`, so the pass is not a fallback. Removing the clamp
;; is REFUTED. That refuting case is committed as crux-status-unclamped.llmll,
;; because naming a refuting case in a comment is not running one.

(module adjudicate

  ;; `c` is the code the console loop carries out of its Done state. The port
  ;; projects it and passes it here as a scalar: a `:status` function is a
  ;; strict-core `def` and may call only body-faithful functions, while every
  ;; accessor in the port is a `def-shell` over a composite state and falls back
  ;; (PROC-BOUNDARY-1 records the same shape). Taking an int keeps the range
  ;; obligation PROVED rather than asserted.
  (def status-of [c: int] -> int
    (post (and (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
               (and (=> (< c 0) (= result 1))
                    (and (=> (> c 255) (= result 255))
                         (=> (and (>= c 0) (<= c 255)) (= result c))))))
    (if (< c 0) 1 (if (> c 255) 255 c))))
